     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">(Left) Einosio Banze, 24, a faecal sludge operator working for ACADEC, a community-based organization contracted by Water and Sanitation for the Urban Poor (WSUP), and his colleague Lino LuÃ­s Nhandimo, 22, clean and deactivate an informal latrine at a house in Maputo, Mozambique, Wednesday 27 September 2017.&#xA;&#xA;â€œI am a Faecal Sludge operator because I did not have a job. I was a small businessman before but the business failed. I used to polish the nails of girlsâ€ says Einosio, 24, â€œmy wife knows about this job, she recognise that there is not many opportunities in this country and this city. There are many people who say this job is not good. If I listen to them then I will have nothing to provide for my family.â€&#xA;&#xA;â€œI usually wake up before seven, then I help my wife with some of the house chores. I go to work and meet my colleagues. I wear boots, gloves an overall as well as gloves and a helmet. If I use gloves I will protect myself. I never work without using gloves. There is nothing that I love about this job, I have a family to provide for. That is the only thing that makes me like this work. I am proud to help people with sanitation. I help them to avoid having diseases, if the toilets are dirty it can cause problems for their health. I have never been sick because of this job. This job can be dangerous, the faeces can enter my mouth. That is why I always wear a mask to avoid the faeces to enter.â€&#xA;&#xA;Water-borne diseases and diseases associated with poor sanitation and hygiene are a significant problem in Mozambique. For example, in 2015 there were 8,858 cholera cases and 65 deaths registered in 21 districts of six provinces. Young children are particularly at risk. Despite reductions in child mortality, 97 children under 5 die per 1,000 live births, and diarrhoeal diseases remain one of the leading causes of death among children. Moreover, evidence suggests that WASH is an essential intervention to reduce stunting; 43 per cent of children under 5 are severely or moderately stunted in Mozambique.&#xA;&#xA;Women and girls are particularly vulnerable due to lack of WASH services. For example, girls lose time at school as they can be required to collect water, sometimes taking up to two hours of their day, and they are especially at risk of sexual assault if they have to go to the toilet in the bush at night. And children with disabilities can be denied access to a school education when accessible WASH facilities are unavailable or inadequate.&#xA;&#xA;Only 25% of Mozambiqueâ€™s urban residents have access to piped water and 56% lack even the most basic sanitation facilities, according to Water and Sanitation for the Urban Poor. As Mozambiqueâ€™s population rises over the coming decades, with most of the growth likely to come in urban areas, there is an urgent need to build better services in Maputo and other cities in the country.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
