<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="Adobe XMP Core 6.0-c002 79.164488, 2020/07/10-22:06:53        ">
 <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
  <rdf:Description rdf:about=""
    xmlns:Iptc4xmpCore="http://iptc.org/std/Iptc4xmpCore/1.0/xmlns/"
    xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
    xmlns:exif="http://ns.adobe.com/exif/1.0/"
    xmlns:photoshop="http://ns.adobe.com/photoshop/1.0/"
    xmlns:xmpRights="http://ns.adobe.com/xap/1.0/rights/"
   Iptc4xmpCore:CountryCode="BOL"
   Iptc4xmpCore:Location="Monteagudo"
   dc:format="image/jpeg"
   exif:DateTimeOriginal="2015-07-16T18:30:00"
   photoshop:City="Maychamayu"
   photoshop:Country="Bolivia"
   photoshop:Credit="Â© UNICEF/UNI189329/Gilbertson VII Photo"
   photoshop:DateCreated="2015-07-16T18:30:00.71"
   photoshop:Source="VII Photo"
   photoshop:State="Chuquisaca Department"
   xmpRights:Marked="True">
   <Iptc4xmpCore:CreatorContactInfo
    Iptc4xmpCore:CiEmailWork="gilbertson@viiphoto.com"
    Iptc4xmpCore:CiTelWork="+12126710406"
    Iptc4xmpCore:CiUrlWork="www.viiphoto.com"/>
   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Ashley Gilbertson / VII Photo</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">On 16 July, Andronico Vargas, using an illustrated UNICEF pamphlet as an aid, teaches his daughters â€“ (left-right) Lucilda, 6, Rilda, 4, and Nelsy, 2 â€“ about toilet usage, in the bathroom of their home, on the outskirts of Monteagudo in Chuquisaca Department. Mr. Vargas, who is a plumber, also works on community water projects.&#xA;&#xA;In 2015, 663 million people do not have access to improved drinking water sources although the global target for safe drinking water was met in 2010 â€“ well ahead of the Millennium Development Goal (MDG) to reduce by half the proportion of the population without safe drinking water and basic sanitation by 2015. Over 90 per cent of the worldâ€™s population now has access to improved sources of drinking water. However, 2.4 billion still lack improved sanitation facilities and 946 million still practise open defecation. Access to safe water and sanitation is a human right, and water and sanitation are fundamental to human development and well-being, as well as critical to achieving other development objectives. The world has made significant progress in water and sanitation but challenges persist, and much remains to be done to ensure that everyone is reached. Inequalities in access to drinking water and sanitation exist, including between rural and urban areas and the rich and the poor, and also include the gender burden of water collection; and the persistent exclusion of the poor from water and sanitation services. In Bolivia, where economic growth and government investment in basic social services continue to improve the lives of children and families, many of the Millennium Development Goals have been achieved and a number of others are on track. Increased safe water and sanitation access and programmes helping to address poverty, equity gaps and disparities faced by the countryâ€™s vulnerable inhabitants are among notable progress that has been achieved, but challenges remain. UNICEF, with the World Health Organization, has initiated the debate about future targets for water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH) and is supporting interventions to address barriers and bottlenecks and improve access and quality of basic social services for vulnerable communities. In Bolivia, UNICEF is supporting increased access to water and sanitation facilities, including in sparsely populated rural areas, and is working with local authorities to provide WASH services for families and communities.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
   <dc:rights>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Â© 2015 Ashley Gilbertson / VII Photo</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:rights>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>1 to 5 years old</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>6 to 11 years old</rdf:li>
