<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="Adobe XMP Core 6.0-c002 79.164488, 2020/07/10-22:06:53        ">
 <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
  <rdf:Description rdf:about=""
    xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
    xmlns:exif="http://ns.adobe.com/exif/1.0/"
    xmlns:photoshop="http://ns.adobe.com/photoshop/1.0/"
   dc:format="image/jpeg"
   exif:DateTimeOriginal="2009-05-07T09:59:54"
   photoshop:ColorMode="3"
   photoshop:Country="Afghanistan"
   photoshop:Credit="Â© UNICEF/UNI62693/Noorani"
   photoshop:DateCreated="2009-05-07"
   photoshop:ICCProfile="Adobe RGB (1998)">
   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Shehzad Noorani</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Women mix cement at the Womenâ€™s Latrine Production Centre in Khwaja Surmaq Village in Anjil District in Herat Province. Khwaja Surmaq, equipped with safe water and sanitary latrines, is a UNICEF-supported â€˜clean villageâ€™. It also has the only womenâ€™s sanitary latrine production centre in the country. UNICEF supports the Centre in partnership with the local NGO Neday-e Zan (The Voice of Women), which implements water and sanitation initiatives in the village and promotes improved hygiene practices and the use of sanitary household latrines in the province. Seven female masons and one male mason have been trained in latrine production. Shafiqa (second from left), who has seven children, earns 100 Afs (about US $1.50) a day at the Centre, funds that are crucial for her familyâ€™s survival. Her husband lost a leg and both hands in an accident and can no longer work. â€œI joined the production centre as soon as I found out about it. I am so happy to have this job,â€ she said. â€œPeople tell me that this is not a womanâ€™s work, but I donâ€™t agree. Do you see anything wrong with the blocks I made? If men can do this, so can I.â€ Shafiqaâ€™s two sons both attend school, but her school-age daughters stay at home to help with the household chores.&#xA;&#xA;In May 2009 in Afghanistan, efforts to rebuild the countryâ€™s infrastructure and systems (largely destroyed during more than two decades of war) and increased access to basic health services have contributed to a decline in child mortality and increased primary school enrolment, especially for girls. Nevertheless, challenges persist. Drought, food shortages, chronic insecurity and ongoing conflict continue to limit progress for the countryâ€™s approximately 27.1 million people. Afghanistan remains one of the poorest countries in the world and one of only four polio-endemic countries worldwide. An estimated 45 per cent of the population is food insecure or borderline, and 40-55 per cent of children are underweight or stunted. Maternal mortality rates (estimated at 1,600 per 100,000 live births) are among the worldâ€™s highest. Only 22 per cent of the population has access to safe drinking water and just 30 per cent, to adequate sanitation. UNICEF, working with the Government, other UN agencies and NGO partners, supports health, nutrition, water, sanitation and hygiene (WASH), education, child protection, and emergency interventions, including in drought-affected, displaced and other vulnerable communities.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>group (people)</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>latrine</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>rural area</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>UNICEF</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>women</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <photoshop:SupplementalCategories>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>AFG-2009-00238.jpg</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </photoshop:SupplementalCategories>
  </rdf:Description>
