<x:xmpmeta xmlns:x="adobe:ns:meta/" x:xmptk="Adobe XMP Core 6.0-c002 79.164488, 2020/07/10-22:06:53        ">
 <rdf:RDF xmlns:rdf="http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#">
  <rdf:Description rdf:about=""
    xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/"
    xmlns:exif="http://ns.adobe.com/exif/1.0/"
    xmlns:photoshop="http://ns.adobe.com/photoshop/1.0/"
   dc:format="image/jpeg"
   exif:DateTimeOriginal="2020-10-01T08:31:30"
   photoshop:ColorMode="3"
   photoshop:Country="Kenya"
   photoshop:Credit="Â© UNICEF/UN0348926/Modola"
   photoshop:DateCreated="2020-10-01T08:31:30+01:00"
   photoshop:ICCProfile="Adobe RGB (1998)"
   photoshop:LegacyIPTCDigest="582A1274C08AC653DDE2FDD62E80932D">
   <dc:creator>
    <rdf:Seq>
     <rdf:li>Siegfried Modola</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Seq>
   </dc:creator>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">On 1 October 2020 in Kenya, Josephine Lea Muhonja (left) 45, poses with her grandson Moses Kwauje, 10, next to a Fresh Life toilet in their plot in Mukuru kwa Ruben, an informal settlement on the outskirts of Nairobi. Josephine has lived in this plot with the rest of her family for the last four years. She explains the importance of having a Fresh Life toilet, provided by sanitation company Sanergy, in her plot. â€œIf this toilet wasn't here it would be difficult for myself and my family to live here,â€ she says. â€œIn this slum, sanitation is a big issue. In the past, many people were getting sick from water-borne diseases but since Sanergy has started putting in these kind of toilets, things have gotten much better. The toilets are well maintained, cleaned and emptied regularly.â€ Nairobi's sewer infrastructure was originally designed to support 800,000 people; the city's population now exceeds 4 million. Some 80% of households in the city do not have access to sewer lines.&#xA;&#xA;Sanergy provides non-sewered (pipeless) sanitation solutions that serve urban residents in informal settlements, at a cost five times cheaper than sewered systems. The company creates a broader network through franchising to localities, thereby providing the resources and tools through which the larger community is enabled to invest in itself. With a urine-diverting squat plate, the high-quality units source-separate solid and liquid waste, making daily collection quick, safe and easy. The waste is then converted at a centralized facility into valuable end-products such as organic fertilizer and insect-based animal feed.&#xA;&#xA;Sanitation is more than just toilets â€“ it is a comprehensive cross-cutting combination of behaviours and facilities joined together to reduce human exposure to diseases by providing availability of â€“ and access to â€“ clean environs. Every day, more than 800 children die from preventable diseases caused by poor water and a lack of access to sanitation and hygiene facilities. Sustainable Development Goal (SDG) 6 commits the world to achieving basic sanitation for all by 2030. UNICEF aims to achieve this by telling the stories of children and families around the world whose lives are impacted by issues surrounding sanitation. Access to safe water, sanitation and hygiene is a human right, essential for the fulfillment of child rights and good physical, mental and social well-being. It is vital to health, child development, and social and economic progress. Safe disposal of excrement and hygienic behaviours are essential for the dignity, status and wellbeing of every person, be they rich or poor, irrespective of whether they live in rural areas, small towns or urban centres. Clean water, basic toilets and good hygiene practices, such as washing hands with soap after defecation, or after cleaning childrenâ€™s bottoms after their defecation, are essential for the survival and development of children. Today, there are about 2.4 billion people who lack improved [sources that, by nature of their construction or through active intervention, are protected from outside contamination, particularly faecal matter. â€“ World Health Organization (WHO)] sanitation, and 663 million with no access to improved water sources. Diarrhoea is one of the most common and preventable disease caused by poor sanitation and a contaminated drinking water supply. Most of the diseases which result in diarrhoea are spread by disease-causing organisms found in human faeces and urine. Such transmission, in which some of the faeces of an infected individual are carried to the mouth of a new host through one of a variety of routes, is by far the most significant transmission mechanism of diarrhoea, accounting for most cases as well as a large proportion of intestinal worm infections. The most effective way of reducing disease transmission is to erect â€œprimaryâ€ barriers which prevent pathogens from entering the environment. This can be done by providing safe and clean toilet facilities and promoting hand-washing with soap. Constructing sanitation facilities can prevent the spread of disease by flies and the contamination of drinking water, fields and living areas. Without these basic needs, the lives of millions of children are at risk. For children under five, water- and sanitation-related diseases are one of the leading causes of death. In times of crisis, children are particularly vulnerable. UNICEF responds and provides emergency relief to those in need.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>WASH</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li>water and sanitation</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
